From 9cb604f516043e181b83361caa1a7d605215dff6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Richard DEMONGEOT <richard@rdem-systems.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 10:14:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/15] machinebackuplib: fix the reader/uploader handoff and
 vm backup-id checks

- BackupFileDevice received the reader's terminal result after
  uploadWorker had already consumed it, so every successful raw
  file/device backup hung forever; on a read error nothing was sent at
  all and the partial index was committed. The Linux/Windows whole-disk
  paths raced the caller against uploadWorker for the same value, so a
  reader failure could also let a partial index be committed.
  uploadWorker is now the only consumer: the reader sends exactly once
  after closing ch, uploadWorker waits for it once ch is drained and
  returns it. The raw reader also stops when the uploader gives up, and
  a failing worker no longer strands the other seven.
- A "vm" snapshot's backup-id must be the numeric VM ID, but that was
  only checked after every disk had been uploaded. Validate it up front.
- gui: multi-disk machine backups went through the per-folder grouping,
  which split the disks into separate snapshots with "<id>_<device>"
  (non-numeric) IDs. Machine backups are never split now.

uploadWorker's workers also shared one `err` variable across eight
goroutines (a failure could be overwritten by nil), left the assignment
mutex locked when the size check failed, and leaked on the first error
(double sends, a dispatcher blocked forever). Rewrite the loop with a
first-error-wins cancel channel: the dispatcher stops, ch2 is closed,
every worker exits and is joined before uploadWorker returns; errors are
worker-local and the mutex is always released. Drop the write-only
`digests` map.
